To blend multiple audio files using Java, you can use the popular open-source library called SoX (Simple Operations on Audio). Here's how you can use it:

1. First, download the necessary SoX libraries for your operating system from their website (https://sox.sourceforge.net/download.html) and include them in your project's classpath.

2. Next, create a new Java class named `AudioBlender` with the following code:

```java
import org.sox4j.*;
import java.io.File;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class AudioBlender {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        List<String> inputFiles = getInputFiles(); // gets list of input audio files
        String outputFile = "output.wav"; // name of output audio file

        SoxRewriter rewriter = new SoxRewriter()
            .input("in")
            .inputs(inputFiles)
            .outputFormat(SoxFormat.WAV)
            .output(outputFile);

        // add volume adjustment
        rewriter.addEffect(new VolumeEffect(-3));
        // add fade in/out effect
        rewriter.addEffect(new FadeEffect(500));

        // start blending process
        rewriter.run();

        System.out.println("Audio blended successfully!");
    }

    private static List<String> getInputFiles() {
        List<String> inputFiles = new ArrayList<>();
        inputFiles.add("birds.wav");
        inputFiles.add("leaves.wav");
        inputFiles.add("cicadas.wav");
        inputFiles.add("children_playing.wav");
        return inputFiles;
    }
}
```

In this code, we first define an array list `inputFiles` containing the names of all our input audio files. We then create a new instance of `SoxRewriter`, which is the core component for blending audio files using SoX. We set the input format to `"in"` and specify the input files using the `inputs()` method

Unfortunately, JSON is a data interchange format and does not have a built-in sorting functionality. However, you can sort an object with its key values using JavaScript's `Object.entries()` and `Array.from()` methods, followed by the spread operator (`...`) and the `sort()` method.

Here's a simple implementation:

```javascript
const sortJSONByKeys = (obj, keys) => {
  return Object.fromEntries(Array.from(Object.entries(obj)).sort(([keyA], [keyB]) => {
    const indexA = keys.indexOf(keyA);
    const indexB = keys.indexOf(keyB);

    if (indexA === -1 && indexB === -1) {
      // If neither key is in the sort order list, use the original ordering
      return 0;
    } else if (indexA === -1 || indexB === -1) {
      // If only one key is in the sort order list, put it before the others
      return indexA > indexB ? 1 : -1;
    } else {
      // Otherwise, compare the indices of the keys in the sort order list
      return indexA - indexB;
    }
  }));
};

// Example usage
const myObj = { b: 2, a: 1, c: 3 };
const sortOrder = ['a', 'c'];
const sortedObj = sortJSONByKeys(myObj, sortOrder);
console.log(sortedObj);
// Output: { a: 1, c: 3, b: 2 }
```

In this example, the `sortJSONByKeys()` function takes an object and an array of keys as arguments. It converts the object into an array of key-value pairs using `Object.entries()`, converts that array back into an object using `Object.fromEntries()`, and then sorts it based on the indices of the specified keys in the sort order list. The original ordering is preserved for any keys that are not included in the sort order list.

