import java.util.InputMismatchException;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);

        int weight = 0;
        int height = 0;
        float bmi = 0;

        try {
            System.out.print("Enter your weight in kilograms: ");
            weight = input.nextInt(); // read weight
            System.out.print("Enter your height in meters: ");
            height = input.nextInt(); // read height
            input.nextLine(); // consume newline character left by previous nextInt() call
            bmi = (float) weight / Math.pow(height, 2); // calculate BMI
            System.out.printf("Your BMI is: %.2f", bmi); // display result with two decimal places
        } catch (InputMismatchException e) { // handle non-integer input
            System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter integers only.");
            input.nextLine(); // consume remaining input on current line
            main(args); // recursively call main method to restart program
        }

        input.close();
    }
}
```

In this modified version of your code, I added an import for `InputMismatchException`. Then, I wrapped the input reading and BMI calculation inside a `try` block to catch the exception when the user enters invalid input. If the input is not an integer, we print an error message and consume the remaining input on the current line using `input.nextLine()`. We then call the `main()` function recursively to restart the program. This way, the user will be prompted to enter valid input again.

