best_response:
 Here's an implementation in TypeScript:

```typescript
type Item = { name: string; position: number; randomize?: boolean };

function shuffleArray(arr: Item[]): Item[] {
  const result: Item[] = [];

  for (let I = 0; I < arr.length; i++) {
    const item = arr[i];

    if (!item.randomize) {
      result[item.position - 1] = item;
    } else {
      result.push(item);
    }
  }

  return result;
}

const fruits: Item[] = [
  { name: "apple", position: 1, randomize: false },
  { name: "pineapple", position: 2, randomize: false },
  { name: "orange", position: 3, randomize: true },
  { name: "banana", position: 4, randomize: true },
  { name: "grapes", position: 8, randomize: false },
  { name: "lemon", position: 11, randomize: true },
  { name: "jack fruit", position: 10, randomize: false },
  { name: "dragon fruit", position: 5, randomize: false },
  { name: "green apple", position: 9, randomize: false },
];

const shuffledFruits = shuffleArray(fruits);
console.log(shuffledFruits);
```

The `shuffleArray()` function takes an array of items and returns a new array with items arranged according to their positions and randomization settings. If `randomize` is false, the item is added to the resulting array at its specified position. Otherwise, it's added randomly. The function uses a simple loop to iterate over the input array and add each item to the output array accordingly. The resulting array is then returned. You can use this function to shuffle your array based on the given criteria. In our example, we pass the `fruits` array to the `shuffleArray()` function and log the result to console. This will output the shuffled array with items arranged based on their positions and randomization settings

To retrieve the username from the JWT (JSON Web Token) in an ASP.NET Core WebAPI, you can follow these steps:

1. Install the `Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens` package by running the following command in your project's terminal or command prompt:

   ```
   dotnet add package Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens
   ```

2. Add the `AddJwtBearer()` method to the `ConfigureServices()` method of your Startup class to configure JWT authentication:

   ```csharp
   services.AddAuthentication(options =>
   {
       options.DefaultAuthenticateScheme = JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme;
       options.DefaultChallengeScheme = JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme;
   })
   .AddJwtBearer(options =>
   {
       options.TokenValidationParameters = new TokenValidationParameters
       {
           ValidateIssuerSigningKey = true,
           IssuerSigningKey = new SymmetricSecurityKey(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("super-secret-key")),
           ValidateIssuer = false,
           ValidateAudience = false
       };
   });
   ```

   In this example, replace "super-secret-key" with your own secret key that will be used to sign and verify the JWT tokens. You can also customize other parameters such as the issuer and audience if needed.

3. Next, add the `[Authorize]` attribute to your controller or action methods to require authentication:

   ```csharp
   [Authorize]
   [Route("api/[controller]/[action]")]
   public IActionResult Get()
   {
       // Retrieve the username from the context
       var username = User.Claims.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Type == ClaimTypes.Name).Value;

       // Return a response
       return Ok();
   }
   ```

   Inside the action method, you can use the `User` property of the controller base class `ControllerBase` to access the claims of the authenticated user. The `ClaimsPrincipal.Current` property can also be used in non-controller classes.

