It's possible that the server is configured to allow CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) requests with wildcard `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` headers, which means any origin can make requests to your server. This allows for greater flexibility in client-side development, as it eliminates the need for pre-flight requests and simplifies the process of making cross-domain requests.

However, when using `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`, it also means that your server doesn't restrict which origins can access your resources, which could potentially lead to security issues if an unauthorized third party tries to access sensitive data. In such cases, you might want to restrict the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header to specific domains or subdomains instead of using a wildcard.

Regarding your issue with including the Authorization header, since your server has already allowed CORS requests from all origins using wildcard `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`, there shouldn't be any restriction on including the Authorization header in the request. You should be able to add the Authorization header to your Axios request just like any other request, as follows:

```javascript
axios.get('https://your-server.com/api/resource', {
