Here's the R code that checks if all non-NA elements in a character vector can be converted to numeric:

```r
check_numeric <- function(x) {
  nm <- !is.na(as.numeric(x))   # Convert x to numeric and check for NA values
  all(nm)                        # Check if all elements of nm are TRUE
}

# Example usage:
my_vector <- c("1", "2.5", "3", "4", "NA", "5")
if (check_numeric(my_vector)) {
  print("All elements of my_vector can be converted to numeric.")
} else {
  print("Not all elements of my_vector can be converted to numeric.")
}
```

The `check_numeric()` function takes an input vector `x` and returns a logical value indicating whether all non-NA elements in `x` can be converted to numeric. The function first converts `x` to numeric using `as.numeric()` and then checks for NA values using `!is.na()`. Finally, it uses the `all()` function to check if all elements of the resulting boolean vector are TRUE. If all non-NA elements can be converted to numeric, the function returns TRUE; otherwise, it returns FALSE. You can use this function to quickly check if a character vector is suitable for numerical analysis without having to handle potential conversion errors manually.

Here's an implementation of the requested decorator using NestJS:

```typescript
import { Injectable } from '@nestjs/common';
import { ExecutionContext, NestMiddleware } from '@nestjs/core';

@Injectable()
export class DevModeMiddleware implements NestMiddleware {
  use(context: ExecutionContext, next: () => any) {
    const req = context.switchToHttp().getRequest();

    if (req.app.get('env') === 'development') {
      return next();
    } else {
      return false;
    }
  }
}

// Example usage:
@AppController()
class AppController {
  constructor(private readonly app: AppService) {}

  @Get()
  @UseGuards(AuthGuard('jwt')) // Authentication guard
  @UseInterceptors(LoggerInterceptor) // Request logging interceptor
  @DevModeMiddleware() // Development mode middleware
  getHello(): string {
    return this.app.getHello();
  }
}
```

In this implementation, we create a new middleware called `DevModeMiddleware`. It takes the `ExecutionContext` and a callback function `next`, which is called to pass control to the next middleware or controller.

The middleware checks the value of the `NODE_ENV` environment variable, which can be accessed through the application instance passed to the controller constructor. If the environment is set to "development", we call the `next()` callback to proceed with the request handling. Otherwise, we immediately return `false`, which prevents further middleware execution and returns a falsey value that can be checked by the user.

You can use this middleware in your controllers by applying it as a decorator using `@DevModeMiddleware()`, as shown in the example above. This will wrap the controller method with the middleware, which will be executed before the method is invoked.

