Here's an example SQL database design to fulfill your requirements:

Table: Books (book_id PK, title, publication_date, publisher, ISBN, summary, author_id FK, genre_id FK)

Table: Authors (author_id PK, first_name, last_name)

Table: Genres (genre_id PK, name)

The Books table links both Author and Genre tables through foreign keys (FK). This ensures that we don't have duplicate data, as each book can only belong to one author and one genre at a time. By storing this information in separate tables, it also makes searching and querying the database more efficient.

Some sample SQL queries to demonstrate functionality:

1. Retrieve all books sorted by title:
   SELECT * FROM Books ORDER BY title;

2. Retrieve all books published after 2015:
   SELECT * FROM Books WHERE publication_date > '2015-01-01';

3. Retrieve all books written by Jane Doe:
   SELECT * FROM Books WHERE author_id = (SELECT author_id FROM Authors WHERE first_name = 'Jane' AND last_name = 'Doe');

4. Retrieve all books belonging to the mystery genre:
   SELECT * FROM Books WHERE genre_id = (SELECT genre_id FROM Genres WHERE name = 'Mystery');

5. Display the total number of books for each genre:
   SELECT Genres.name AS genre, COUNT(Books.book_id) AS num_books FROM Genres LEFT JOIN Books ON Genres.genre_id = Books.genre_id GROUP BY Genres.genre_id;

6. Update the summary of a specific book:
   UPDATE Books SET summary = 'New Summary' WHERE book_id = 123;

7. Delete a book from the database:
   DELETE FROM Books WHERE book_id = 456;

These queries illustrate how users can search, filter, sort, and manipulate their stored book data using SQL commands. The database design and implementation ensure efficiency and reduce redundancy while providing necessary functionality.

